    <h2>The signs you've got a blockage — not just a slow drain</h2>
    <p>A slow sink is annoying. These mean the problem is already downstream and getting worse:</p>
    <ul class="checklist">
      <li>Gurgling from the sink or shower when the toilet flushes — air being forced back through the traps.</li>
      <li>Water backing up in the lowest drain in the house, usually the shower.</li>
      <li>A gully trap outside overflowing onto the lawn.</li>
      <li>More than one fixture slow at the same time — that's the main drain, not the plughole.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>What we actually do</h3>
    <p>We start with the drain machine (the "electric eel") at the nearest access point. If the blockage won't shift or keeps coming back, a CCTV drain camera finds the cause — usually tree roots, a bellied pipe, or decades of fat. You see the footage yourself; nobody's asking you to take our word for what's down there.</p>
    <h3>Before we arrive</h3>
    <p>Stop running water into the blocked line. If sewage is backing up indoors, keep everyone off the wet area and shut lids. Don't pour caustic drain cleaner in — it doesn't clear main-line blockages and it makes the pipe dangerous for whoever opens it.</p>
